<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Old Kingdom of Egypt</h1><p class="st-meta">2686 BCE – 2181 BCE (approx.) · capital Memphis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Egypt of the Third to Sixth Dynasties, the age of the pyramids and of an all-powerful god-king ruling from Memphis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Old Kingdom began around 2686 BCE with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-egypt">Third Dynasty</a>, which followed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-dynastic-egypt">Early Dynastic Egypt</a>. Its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a> and his official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-djoser">Step Pyramid</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>, the first monumental stone building, and the kings of the Fourth Dynasty turned the experiment into true pyramids.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a> built the pyramids at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meidum">Meidum</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a> raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menkaure">Menkaure</a>. A literate bureaucracy headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> taxed grain and organised labour on a vast scale. The Fifth Dynasty favoured the sun god Ra with sun temples, and Unas had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a> carved in his tomb. Expeditions sought turquoise in Sinai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the Sixth Dynasty, provincial governors (nomarchs) grew powerful and hereditary, and the very long reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-ii">Pepi II</a> was followed by weakness, possibly worsened by low <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> floods. Central authority faded around 2181 BCE, leaving the short-lived <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eighth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighth Dynasty of Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/old-kingdom-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Old Kingdom of Egypt" width="320" height="581"><figcaption>Image: Cattette · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:4th_Dynasty_of_Egypt-03.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2686 BCE – 2181 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Memphis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Djoser, Khufu, Pepi II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Egyptian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Egyptian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Egypt</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Old Kingdom, Third to Sixth Dynasties, Age of the Pyramids</dd></dl></aside></div>
